Victims of violence against women and their children have the following rights under RA 9262:
(a) to be treated with respect and dignity;
(b) to avail of legal assistance from the Public Attorney's Office (PAO) of the Department of Justice (DOJ) or any public legal assistance office;
(c) to be entitled to support services from the Department of Social Welfare and Development (DSWD) and local government units (LGUs);
(d) to be entitled to all legal remedies and support as provided for under the Family Code; and
(e) to be informed of their rights and the services available to them including their right to apply for a protection order [RA 9262, Section 35].

The Implementing Rules and Regulations (IRR) reiterate these rights with the same details [IRR, Rule VI, Section 38].

The Secretariat to the Council has the following responsibilities:
- Coordinate and monitor, under the direction of the Council, the implementation of the policies and guidelines promulgated by the Council;
- Assist in establishing, maintaining and managing a central database on VAWC, wherever feasible;
- Provide technical, secretariat and records keeping and other services to the Council; and
- Perform such other functions as may be directed by the Council [IRR, Rule IX, Section 58].

NGOs working on violence against women and their children (VAWC) can help implement the law by:

- Formulating and implementing policies and programs to eliminate VAWC;
- Conducting capacity-building activities for government personnel and sharing their experiences in handling VAWC cases;
- Coordinating with concerned agencies, local government units (LGUs), and other NGOs in reporting alleged perpetrators, rescuing victim-survivors, and conducting investigations if necessary;
- Documenting VAWC cases;
- Disseminating related policies and guidelines to eliminate VAWC to their networks locally and internationally; and
- Providing services to VAWC victim-survivors and their families.

Additionally, NGO-government partnerships and joint projects are encouraged to promote these objectives effectively [IRR, Rule IX, Section 60].

The Department of Health (DOH) shall provide medical assistance to victims of violence against women and their children [RA 9262, Section 40]. Specifically, through the Women and Children Protection Unit (WCPU) in DOH-retained hospitals or in coordination with LGUs or other government health facilities, the following health programs and services shall be immediately provided through a socialized scheme:

a) Complete physical and mental examinations;
b) Medical/surgical treatment;
c) Psychological and psychiatric evaluation and treatment;
d) Hospital confinement when necessary;
e) Referral to specialty hospitals and other concerned agencies as needed;
f) Management of reproductive health concerns of victim-survivors; and
g) If necessary, contact the DSWD or social worker of the LGU for emergency assistance to the woman and her child/children, or the police women and children concerns protection desk officer [IRR, Rule VI, Section 40].

Violence against women and their children under the law includes the following acts:

(a) Causing physical harm to the woman or her child;
(b) Threatening to cause the woman or her child physical harm;
(c) Attempting to cause the woman or her child physical harm;
(d) Placing the woman or her child in fear of imminent physical harm;
(e) Attempting to compel or compelling the woman or her child to engage in conduct which they have the right to desist from, or to desist from conduct which they have the right to engage in, or attempting to restrict their freedom of movement or conduct by force, threat of force, physical or other harm, or intimidation. This includes threatening or depriving custody or access to family, depriving financial support, depriving legal rights, preventing engagement in legitimate activities, or controlling money or properties;
(f) Inflicting or threatening to inflict physical harm on oneself to control the woman's actions or decisions;
(g) Causing or attempting to cause the woman or her child to engage in any sexual activity which does not constitute rape, by force, threat, physical harm, or intimidation;
(h) Engaging in conduct that alarms or causes substantial emotional or psychological distress, including stalking, peering or lingering outside residence, entering property against will, destroying property or harming pets, and harassment or violence;
(i) Causing mental or emotional anguish, public ridicule or humiliation, including repeated verbal and emotional abuse, and denial of financial support or custody or access to children [RA 9262, Section 5]; [IRR, Rule III, Section 7].

These acts encompass physical, sexual, psychological, and economic abuse as defined in the law [RA 9262, Section 3]; [IRR, Rule II, Section 5].

A petition for protection order may be filed by any of the following:

(a) the offended party;
(b) parents or guardians of the offended party;
(c) ascendants, descendants or collateral relatives within the fourth civil degree of consanguinity or affinity;
(d) officers or social workers of the DSWD or social workers of local government units (LGUs);
(e) police officers, preferably those in charge of women and children’s desks;
(f) Punong Barangay or Barangay Kagawad;
(g) lawyer, counselor, therapist or healthcare provider of the petitioner;
(h) at least two (2) concerned responsible citizens of the city or municipality where the violence against women and their children occurred and who has personal knowledge of the offense committed [RA 9262, Section 9]; this is confirmed by the IRR which lists the same categories [IRR, Rule IV, Section 12].
